## 5. TYPOGRAPHY — RESEARCH-BACKED PAIRINGS

Pick ONE pairing per project based on brand tone. Always: heading font carries
personality, body font carries readability — never use both fonts for both jobs.

| Tone | Heading | Body | Best for |
|---|---|---|---|
| Technical / SaaS default | Inter | Inter (single family, 100–900 weights) | Dashboards, dev tools — most successful SaaS ships one font |
| Modern SaaS, more character | Space Grotesk | Inter | Landing pages wanting distinct display flavor over a legible workhorse |
| 2026-forward / design-aware | Bricolage Grotesque | Inter or Lora | Studio sites, portfolios, brands signaling design maturity |
| Premium / editorial | Instrument Serif | Inter | Hero statements needing a high-end, high-contrast serif moment |
| Classic luxury | Playfair Display | Inter or Source Sans 3 | Agencies, fashion, high-end consumer brands |
| Fintech / consulting (superfamily, zero risk) | IBM Plex Serif | IBM Plex Sans | Products needing coherent, competent, non-flashy trust signals |
| Friendly B2C | Poppins | DM Sans or Inter | Consumer apps wanting warmth over strict geometry |

- Self-host `.woff2` files where possible (better performance, avoids sending
  visitor IPs to Google); otherwise use `next/font/google` which self-hosts
  automatically. Always set `font-display: swap`.
- Type scale: hero `text-5xl md:text-7xl font-semibold tracking-tight`;
  section header `text-3xl md:text-5xl font-semibold`; body
  `text-base md:text-lg text-muted leading-relaxed`.

